-- A QUALIFIED spelling inside a refinement predicate -- `List.length(_)`
-- rather than the bare `len(_)` -- parses, typechecks, and enforces NOTHING.
-- Refinement predicates are never run through the expression desugarer
-- (`Desugar.respan_ty` is the only place that touches `A.TyRefine`, and it
-- only respans), so `List.length` stays an `EField` chain rather than the
-- dotted `EVar` the measure alias keys on. The obligation this contract
-- looks like it generates is silently skipped.
--
-- Until Task 4 of the 2026-07-30 refinement backlog that was SILENT: the
-- contract read as working and did nothing. It now emits a WARNING naming
-- both the qualified spelling found and the bare spelling that works.
--
-- This is an `accept/` witness on purpose, and its exit code is the point.
-- A warning does not change `--check`'s exit status, so this program must
-- stay exit 0: turning the qualified spelling into a hard ERROR is a
-- separate decision (it would break code that compiles today), and this
-- witness is the regression guard against making it one by accident. The
-- warning TEXT is pinned in `test/test_refinecheck.ml`'s `qualified-predicate`
-- suite instead -- `check_types.sh` asserts only exit 0 for `accept/`, and
-- pins substrings for `reject/` alone.
--
-- The bare spelling that DOES enforce is already witnessed by t115-t118,
-- t128 and t130; this program is the negative-space companion to those.
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md, "A qualified spelling in a predicate
-- enforces nothing".
mod T136 do

  fn inner(xs : {List(Int) | List.length(_) > 0}) : Int do
    List.length(xs)
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    inner([1])
  end

end
